hey this is pressure Locker I received  this problem by email because it's going  viral on Facebook with thousands of  people arguing about the correct answer  how smart are you a guy walked into a  store and steals a hundred-dollar bill  from the register without the owner's  knowledge he then buys  $70 worth of goods using the hundred  dollar bill and the owner gives $30 and  change how much money did the owner lose  was a thirty dollar seventy dollars a  hundred dollars one hundred thirty  dollars one hundred seventy dollars or  two hundred dollars can you figure it  out give this problem a try and when  you're ready keep watching the video for  the correct answer in some math problems  and in this story it helps to break the  problem down into different parts so at  first we'll only consider the first part  of the story a guy walks the new store  and feels a hundred dollar bill from the  register without the owner's knowledge  at this point how much does the owner  lose everyone agrees the owner loses a  hundred dollars the owner is minus 100  from the initial part of the story  so now let's separately consider just  the second part of the story  imagine someone buy $70 worth of goods  using a hundred dollar bill and the  owner gives $30 and change how much  would you say the owner has lost in just  this part of the story well if someone  buys $10 worth of goods using $100 bill  you would expect the owner to give $30  and change in fact the owner is  overseeing this transaction so the owner  here is not losing any money the owner  is you could say zero in this  transaction so now we put these two  stories together  the total loss will be the sum of these  two parts of the story we can add  negative 100 and zero to get that the  total loss is $100  the owner is negative 100 dollars from  before the story started so the correct  answer is the owner lost $100 now  there's one technicality if you want to  really overthink this problem and go  into the accounting details now when  someone buy 70 dollars worth of goods in  a store it's not because the goods are  worth $70 to the owner what happens here  is the owner is getting $100 bill and  gives back 30 dollars and change but the  goods have some value V this is usually  going to be less than $70 so you could  say the owner is actually losing the  value of the goods but then getting  annexed $70 because it's $100 bill - the  $30 and change so here the owner loses V  minus 70 or you could say the owner is  70 minus V in terms of the cash position  so if you were to take these two stories  now you add up the results  the owner ultimately loses $30 plus the  value V of the good so if we assume the  value V is 70 dollars which you say $70  worth of goods then you would say the  owner lost $100 you could still arrive  at the same answer of $100 but this is a  little more accurate in terms of pure  accounting so why are so many people  getting the problem wrong well one of  the common answers is there is a 200  dollar loss to the owner many people are  saying this is because in the first part  of the story the owner loses a hundred  dollars that's stolen and then in the  second part of the story the owner loses  another hundred dollars because the  owner is giving up seven dollars worth  of goods and $30 and change so the  mistake here is that it's not taking a  new account the owner is also getting a  hundred dollar bill so when you take  that other part of the story where you  get the hundred dollar bill that will  just the total by +100 and when you do  that that means the owner ultimately  lost $100 in total  another common answer is that the owner  had $130 loss this is from the stolen  hundred dollar bill and the thirty  dollars and change  well the problem with this answer is  it's not taking account that the owner  also gave up $70 in goods but also got a  hundred dollar bill so when you take  into account these transactions the net  result would be the owner losing $100  which is negative 100  so to summarize most of the incorrect  responses either didn't add or subtract  the numbers correctly or they didn't  take into account every transaction it  seems the fact that in the second half  of the story the person used the stolen  dollar bill somehow people don't treat  that as a genuine hundred dollar bill  that is $100 that the owner is getting  back when you take into account every  single transaction and you treat it  correctly as either a loss or a gain for  the owner you end up that the owner lost  $100 did you figure it out thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel I make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions you can follow on  Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  catch me on social media at pressure  Locker if you liked this video please  check out my book there are links in the  video description
 

